 W7. If I meld T5-T6-T7 as a Chow, I use T5, T6, T7. So leftover tiles: W4 and W7 \\u2014 that's a pair of W4 and W7? No, they're different tiles, so not a pair. I'd need to draw a matching tile for either W4 or W7 to complete a pair. Alternatively, I could aim for Seven Pairs: currently I have 8 tiles, with duplicates: W7 is a pair (two W7s), others are singles: W4, W6, W8, T5, T6, T7. That's only one pair so far, far from Seven Pairs.\\n\\nGiven the unshown tiles count, W4 has 4 left, W6 has 4 left, W7 has 4 left, W8 has 4 left, T5 has 4 left, T6 has 4 left, T7 has 3 left. So many tiles are available.\\n\\nI should consider which discard improves my hand toward a winning shape. Currently, my hand is close to having two additional Chows. I need a pair. The leftover after making those Chows would be W4 and W7 \\u2014 they are not a pair, but if I keep both, I could hope to draw another W4 or W7 to make a pair. Alternatively, I could discard one of them to try to create a better shape.\\n\\nIf I discard T7, I break the T5-T6-T7 Chow possibility, but I still have the W Chow possibility. That might not be good. If I discard W4, I keep the W6-W7-W8 Chow and T5-T6-T7 Chow, leaving W7 as a single \\u2014 then I need a pair of W7 (I already have one W7, so I need one more W7) or another pair. Since I have two W7s, if I discard W4, I have W7, W7 as a pair? Wait, after making W6-W7-W8 Chow, I use one W7, so I'd have one W7 left, not two. So I'd have W7 left and T5, T6, T7 if I don't meld the T Chow? Actually, let's think step by step.\\n\\nOption 1: Aim for standard hand with 4 melds + pair. My existing melds: B5 pung, T7-T8-T9 Chow. I need two more melds from hand. Possible melds in hand:  \\n- Chow W6-W7-W8 (using W6, one W7, W8)  \\n- Chow T5-T6-T7 (using T5, T6, T7)  \\nThen leftover: W4 and the other W7 (since I used one W7 for the W Chow). That's W4 and W7, which are not a pair. So I need to convert one into a pair by drawing a matching tile, or change my plan.\\n\\nMaybe I can use the two W7s as a pair, and then form other melds. If I pair W7, then I need two more melds from: W4, W6, W8, T5, T6, T7. These can form: W4-W5-W6? I don't have W5. W6-W7-W8? But W7 is paired, so not for Chow. T5-T6-T7 is a Chow. So then I'd have: pair W7, Chow T5-T6-T7, and then I need one more meld from W4, W6, W8. These could form a Chow if I get W5 for W4-W5-W6, or W7 for W6-W7-W8 but W7 is paired. So not good.\\n\\nAlternatively, I could aim for a pair from T5, T6, or T7, but they are all singles.\\n\\nConsidering the unshown tiles, W7 has 4 left, but I already have 2, so only 2 more in wall. W4 has 4 left. T7 has 3 left, but I have 1, and one is in my exposed Chow? Actually my exposed Chow uses T7, T8, T9, so that T7 is used, not in hand. So T7 in hand is separate. So T7 has 3 left unshown.\\n\\nMaybe I should keep flexible and discard a tile that is less useful. W4 is isolated, no immediate sequence. W6 and W8 can work with W7. T5, T6, T7 work together. So W4 seems weakest. Discarding W4 keeps the two potential Chows and the W7 pair possibility if I draw another W7? But as said, if I form the W Chow, I use one W7, leaving one W7. So to have a pair, I need to not form the W Chow and instead keep both W7s as pair, but then I need two other melds from W4, W6, W8, T5, T6, T7. That gives T5-T6-T7 as one meld, and then W4, W6, W8 need a W5 to form a Chow with W4-W5-W6, or W7 for W6-W7-W8 but W7 is paired. So that's difficult.\\n\\nMaybe discard W8? Then I have W4, W6, W7, W7, T5, T6, T7. Possible melds: T5-T6-T7 Chow, and then with W tiles: W6-W7-W8? But no W8. W4-W5-W6? Need W5. W7 pair? Then leftover W4, W6 need meld. Not good.\\n\\nDiscard T5 or T6? Breaks T Chow.\\n\\nGiven the exposed melds of opponents: next player has none, across has B1-B2-B3 Chow, previous has B6-B7-B8 Chow. So many bamboo tiles are used. My own melds use B5 and T7-T8-T9. So terminals and simples are in play.\\n\\nI think the best approach is to discard W4, as it is the most isolated tile, and keep the sequences together. Then I can hope to draw W5 or another W7 to improve. Also, discarding W4 might avoid feeding opponents' sequences, as W4 is a simple tile with many left.\\n\\nValid actions: Play T6, T5, T7, W7, W6, W4, W8.\\n\\nReason: Discarding W4 maintains the potential for two Chows (W6-W7-W8 and T5-T6-T7) and keeps the W7 duplicate which could become part of a pair if I draw a third W7 or if I adjust later. It also avoids breaking existing combinations.\\n\\nAnswer: Play W4\"}"}
